this is tomorrow same standing in for  reviewer Richard weakling journey to the  savage planet is a fantastic name for a  pulpy sci-fi game but it's a bit of a  misdirect when taken at face value sure  there are things that want to kill you  in journey to the savage planet but  they're only a minor inconvenience  rather than the main focus instead the  emphasis here is on exploration coupling  this with genuine humour and a charming  tone to present a light-hearted and  singularly focused chunk of sci-fi  adventuring  [Music]  the entire game takes place on a single  planet located deep in uncharted space  you're strapped into the space boots of  an employee of kindred aerospace a  rinky-dink outfit that's proud of its  standing as the fourth best interstellar  exploration company you have to catalog  the flora fauna and life located across  the various biomes of planet ary 26 to  determine if it's fit for human  habitation  what would the whole climate change  ruining earth thing journey to the  savage planet excels when it comes to  the assortment of tools and equipment  you can gradually craft and use to reach  every nook and cranny of the planet  surface you're immediately free to  explore as you see fit but it doesn't  take long to discover plenty of  inaccessible areas as such much of the  game is spent scanning the flora and  fauna to reveal whether they have  gameplay benefits or are just there to  contribute to the planets aesthetic some  plants may contain seeds that restore  your health or produce project our  explosives while alien critters are  filled with resources you can gather if  you're heartless enough to put a laser  blast between their eyes gathering these  resources and locating items that can be  reverse engineered using your ships 3d  printer allows you to craft equipment  like grappling hooks  double jump upgrades for your jet pack  and other tools that make traversal and  deeper exploration possible each new  upgrade opens up more of the planet for  you to probe by the end of the 10-hour  adventure you'll be gliding across  natural zip lines hundreds of feet in  the air  propelling across perilous chasms with a  triple jump and using a powerful ground  pound to unearth new passages journey to  the savage planet adopts the  metroidvania formula and executes it  wonderfully presenting you with an ever  growing arsenal of tools that are  satisfying to use and feed into the  game's inherent focus on exploration of  course the other side of this equation  is the planet itself which is well worth  turning inside out ary 26 is split into  three distinct biomes each one is  moderately sized resulting in the  planets scale feeling manageable and  allowing you to explore freely without  the fear of getting lost when presented  with multiple paths it's easy to choose  one over the other because you know  getting back to that initial fork in the  road is going to be relatively easy this  encourages you to poke your nose in  every crevice travel to every faraway  cave and check behind every  waterfall you're often rewarded for  doing so with extra resources or  important upgrade items hidden  throughout the planet not to mention the  visual trees are on offer in each  disparate biome journey to the savage  planet isn't a completely leisurely tour  though your first order of business is  to develop a futuristic blaster pistol  but combat is a means to an end rather  than a major part of the game and ends  up being a drag defeating any aggressive  predators involves a rinse and repeat  pattern whereby you use a nifty sidestep  or a jump to avoid an attack before  following up by shooting one or multiple  weak points there are only slight  deviations on this back and forth that  require you to lob an explosive or  poison cloud at the enemy before you can  pepper its weak points  the pistol never feels quite accurate  enough for the job especially because  you're usually being asked to hit small  targets and each of the combats faults  come to a head during the game's closing  moments as you're thrown into one fight  after another before facing off against  the final boss you can play the whole  game cooperatively with a friend but  Corp doesn't alter the moment-to-moment  gameplay in any significant way  conflicts are easier with two people  sure but there's nothing about the co-op  experience that's intrinsically built  for more than a single player you can  explore the planet together or Optus  split up and cover different ground but  that's about it playing with a friend  can result in moments of emergent humor  but journey to the savage planet is also  genuinely funny thanks to an abundance  of FMVs located on your ship these show  an incredibly eccentric videos mock and  parody everything from exploitative  corporate practices to the video game  industry the humor is somewhat  front-loaded but this does help the  games irreverent charm establish itself  earlier neater savage planet borrows  plenty of familiar elements from other  games yeah it does so in a carefree way  that sets it apart from other sci-fi  exploration games settling on a relaxing  play style that's informed by its single  vivid planet and tightly focused design  it only takes a couple of hours to  reveal its humdrum combat but this is  the only significant damper on what is  an entertaining slice of light-hearted  planetary exploration  [Music]  you
 

